# Data / models
data/
# Trained model artifacts are per-machine runtime output, not source. The
# anomaly baselines in particular describe what "normal" looks like on ONE
# host — committing them would hand a fresh clone someone else's baseline.
# (*.pt was already ignored here; the .pkl sibling was tracked by accident,
# so every server run dirtied the working tree with a 4MB binary diff.)
ai/models/
*.db
*.sqlite
*.bin
*.pt

# Remote-access state is the same class of thing: one machine's Tailscale
# address and the moment it was last seen, written by network/remote_access.py
# on every connect. It was tracked, so a clone arrived carrying whoever
# committed it last — a real personal IP in the repo, and a URL that
# get_access_info() would hand back to the next user as if it were theirs.
# Absent is already a supported state; it reports "not_connected".
network/remote_access_info.json
*.gguf

